# {{.project_name}}

This is a Skyulf 0.9.0 local-engine Databricks Bundle. The selected fit and
prediction engine is **{{.engine}}**. Spark is used to read bounded Delta rows
and publish the prediction table; Skyulf runs feature engineering and model
prediction on the selected local engine. This project was generated for the
**{{.deployment_mode}}** deployment profile.

## Before the first run

1. Put the matching, trusted `skyulf_core-0.9.0-py3-none-any.whl` in `dist/`.
   From a Skyulf source checkout, run `uv build --wheel --no-build-isolation
   --out-dir <this-project>/dist skyulf-core`. Rebuild the wheel whenever the
   source changes. The jobs also install MLflow 3.16.1.
2. Edit `config/workflow.json`: set real training and scoring tables, features,
   temporal split, preprocessing/model config, memory bounds and the concrete
   training Delta version. The generated values are examples, not a dataset.
3. Create the source and empty prediction Delta tables. Enable Change Data
   Feed on the scoring source **before** new rows arrive. The prediction table
   needs `row_keys`, `prediction`, `run_id`, `model_name`, and `model_version`
   with types matching the Skyulf output. Create the separate score admission
   table with `(target_id STRING, owner STRING)` and one row whose `target_id`
   is the prediction table's Delta identity and `owner` is null. If using the
   stage/promote jobs, create a separate alias admission table with the same
   columns and one row with `target_id` equal to
   `skyulf.integrations.mlflow.promotion.alias_resource_id(model_name)`.
4. Authenticate the Databricks CLI, then validate and deploy the intended
   target with `databricks bundle validate --strict -t <target> --profile
   <profile>` and `databricks bundle deploy -t <target> --profile <profile>`.
   The CLI profile supplies credentials; none are stored here.

{{if eq .deployment_mode "personal"}}
The only target is `dev`, using serverless jobs. The initial catalog and schema
come from the two initialization questions. Use this mode in a personal
workspace for development and isolated live tests.
{{else}}
The company targets are `test`, `syst` and `prod`. They use the approved job
cluster policy and `PayingRegNo` cost tag supplied at initialization. Verify
the workspace host, three catalog mappings, schema names, policy name, tag and
CLI profile with your team before deployment. The questionnaire defaults are
examples from the supplied company reference, not confirmed production values.
`test` adds a per-user suffix to the registered model, prediction and internal
admission tables. `syst` and `prod` use stable names in distinct catalogs.
The input tables are existing tables; edit their full UC names if another team
owns them. If the three targets live in different workspaces, set each target's
`workspace.host` separately before validation.
{{end}}

| Job | When to run | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `train` | New labeled data or model candidate | Fits and registers a candidate, logs held-out MLflow metrics |
| `compare` | Review a candidate | Read-only comparison with the current champion |
| `stage` | After an eligible comparison | Assigns the candidate to `@challenger` |
| `promote` | Explicit release decision | Moves the staged candidate to `@champion` |
| `score` | Each new source increment | Appends only new-row predictions to one target table |

The project uses one prediction table per model. Its score and alias admission
tables are internal coordination tables in the metadata schema, not prediction
outputs. Normal recurring inference runs only `score`; training and alias jobs
have their own release cadence.

Run `databricks bundle run train -t <target>` to train one candidate, log held-out
metrics and register its model. Inspect the resulting model version in Unity
Catalog, and set `model_version` in the JSON to that **concrete version** before
running `databricks bundle run score -t <target>`. Redeploy after changing the
configuration. Each score run handles the initial bounded source snapshot or
only inserts since the last committed receipt. Run it again with no new rows
to see a no-op; no date or source-version argument is needed. Source updates
and deletes require a separate rescore policy.

The first model has no champion by default. Once a champion alias has been
explicitly initialized, set `champion_version` and `candidate_version` to
concrete versions from the **same** pinned labeled holdout. The `compare` job
is read-only. `stage` and `promote` are separate manual jobs: they re-evaluate
the comparison and use the distributed alias admission row. Promotion requires
the matching candidate to have been staged as `@challenger`. Neither training
nor comparison changes aliases automatically. Keep scoring pinned to a
concrete `model_version` until you explicitly decide to move it; an alias
change does not rescore historical rows.

The default jobs have no schedules. Add a scoring schedule after checking the
source append policy and table permissions. Optional retraining schedules,
full-history rescores, Spark feature engineering and endpoints are separate
future extensions.
